= Solution
{parent=a}

In the <cylindrical coordinate system>, $r=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}=5$ and $z=-1$. The point is in the third quadrant, so a representative azimuth in $[0,2\pi)$ is $\pi+\arctan(4/3)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{(r,\theta,z)=\left(5,\ \pi+\arctan\frac43,\ -1\right)}.
$$
The angle is defined modulo $2\pi$.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For <spherical coordinates>, the distance from the origin is $\sqrt{26}$ and the polar angle satisfies $\cos\theta=z/r=-1/\sqrt{26}$. The azimuth is the same as in part (a). Thus
$$
\boxed{(r,\theta,\phi)=\left(\sqrt{26},\ \arccos\left(-\frac1{\sqrt{26}}\right),\ \pi+\arctan\frac43\right)}.
$$
The polar angle is in $(\pi/2,\pi)$ because the point lies below the $xy$-<plane>.

= 3
{parent=Paper 2}
