= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <vector triple product identity> is
$$
\mathbf a\times(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)
=\mathbf b(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf c)-\mathbf c(\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b).
$$
Consequently a choice of the requested <coefficients> is
$$
\boxed{\alpha=0,\qquad\beta=\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf c,\qquad\gamma=-\mathbf a\cdot\mathbf b}.
$$
These are the standard identity's <coefficients>, even if the particular three <vectors> are <linearly dependent> and a representation need not be unique.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <cross product> has magnitude $|\mathbf a||\mathbf b|\sin\theta$, where $\theta$ is their angle. Therefore

**It is the area of the parallelogram spanned by the two vectors.**

The <area> is zero if the <vectors> are parallel or either is zero. The corresponding <triangle> has half this <area>.

= 4
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

The traversal fixes the <orientation> of the <vector area> as half the <cross product> of the edges $AB$ and $AC$:
$$
\mathbf S_{ABCA}=\frac12(1,1,0)^T\times(1,1,1)^T
=\boxed{\frac12(1,-1,0)^T}.
$$
Its <Euclidean norm> $1/\sqrt2$ is the <area> of the <triangle>, and its direction follows the <right-hand rule>.

= 5
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

The <fundamental theorem of calculus> gives $y'(x)=e^{-x^2}>0$, so the graph is strictly increasing. Its second <derivative> is $y''(x)=-2xe^{-x^2}$: it curves upward for $x<0$ and downward for $x>0$, with an <inflection point> at zero.

By the <Gaussian integral> and the evenness of its <integrand>,
$$
\boxed{y(0)=\frac{\sqrt\pi}{2}},\qquad
\lim_{x\to-\infty}y(x)=0,\qquad
\lim_{x\to+\infty}y(x)=\sqrt\pi.
$$
Thus the only axis intersection is $(0,\sqrt\pi/2)$; there is no finite $x$-axis intersection because the <integral> is strictly positive for every finite $x$. The <error function> gives $y(x)=\tfrac{\sqrt\pi}{2}(1+\operatorname{erf}x)$.

\Image[paper-2-q5-gaussian-integral.png]
{title=The increasing integral of a Gaussian}
{description=The graph has its only axis intersection and its <inflection point> at $(0,\sqrt\pi/2)$. The horizontal lines at zero and $\sqrt\pi$ are asymptotes.}

