= a
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For the specified <standard basis> vectors, $\mathbf a\times\mathbf b=\hat{\mathbf k}$, so
$$
(\mathbf a\times\mathbf b)\times\mathbf c=\hat{\mathbf k}\times\hat{\mathbf k}=0.
$$
On the other hand, $\mathbf b\times\mathbf c=\hat{\mathbf i}-\hat{\mathbf j}$ and hence
$$
\mathbf a\times(\mathbf b\times\mathbf c)
=\hat{\mathbf i}\times(\hat{\mathbf i}-\hat{\mathbf j})=-\hat{\mathbf k}.
$$
These results are unequal, proving that the <cross product> does not satisfy the <associative property>.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

If the three <vectors> lie in one <plane> through the origin and $\mathbf u,\mathbf v$ are independent, their <cross product> is a <normal vector> to that <plane>, so its <dot product> with $\mathbf w$ is zero. If $\mathbf u,\mathbf v$ are dependent, their <cross product> is already zero. Thus coplanarity always forces the <scalar triple product> to vanish.

Taking the <contrapositive> proves that a nonzero <scalar triple product> rules out coplanarity. Geometrically its <absolute value> is the nonzero <volume> of the spanned <parallelepiped>.

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use displacements from $P_1$, since coplanarity of points concerns an affine <plane> that need not contain the origin. The three displacement <vectors> are
$$
\mathbf u=(0,1,1)^T,\quad\mathbf v=(1,2,1)^T,\quad\mathbf w=(2,3,2)^T.
$$
Their <cross product> is $\mathbf u\times\mathbf v=(-1,1,-1)^T$ and its <dot product> with $\mathbf w$ is $-1$. Since the <scalar triple product> is nonzero,

**The four points do not lie in a common plane.**

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The three displacements from $Q_1$ are
$$
\mathbf u=(1,1,1)^T,\quad\mathbf v=(-1,2,1)^T,\quad\mathbf w=(0,3,2)^T.
$$
Here $\mathbf u\times\mathbf v=(-1,-2,3)^T$ and its <dot product> with $\mathbf w$ is zero. Because the <cross product> itself is nonzero, the first three points determine a <plane> and the fourth lies on it. Explicitly all four satisfy
$$
\boxed{-x-2y+3z=3}.
$$
Thus **the four points are coplanar**.

= 12
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <stationary point> equations are $ae^x=c$ and $be^y=d$. The sign assumptions make both ratios positive, so each equation has exactly one real solution. Therefore
$$
\boxed{(x_*,y_*)=\left(\log\frac ca,\ \log\frac db\right)}.
$$
Substitution gives the stationary value
$$
\boxed{h_*=c+d-c\log\frac ca-d\log\frac db}.
$$

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <Hessian matrix> is diagonal:
$$
H(x,y)=\begin{pmatrix}ae^x&0\\0&be^y\end{pmatrix},\qquad
H(x_*,y_*)=\begin{pmatrix}c&0\\0&d\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Its <eigenvalues> are $c$ and $d$. Hence the <second-derivative test> gives a strict <local minimum> if $c,d>0$, a strict <local maximum> if $c,d<0$, and a <saddle point of a scalar function> if $cd<0$. No degenerate case occurs because both are nonzero.

The <local minimum> is the unique <global minimum>, since both separated summands are <strictly convex functions> and tend to $+\infty$ at either end of the real line. Similarly, when both signs are negative there is a unique <global maximum>.

= c
{parent=12}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Here the <stationary point> is the origin, of value two. The <Taylor expansion> is
$$
h=2+\frac{x^2+y^2}{2}+\frac{x^3+y^3}{6}+O((|x|+|y|)^4).
$$
Thus nearby <level curves> are approximately concentric circles. Globally the <level curves> above two are closed around the unique <minimum>, symmetric under interchanging $x$ and $y$, but not under changing either coordinate's sign. The faster exponential growth for positive coordinates compresses the curves on their positive sides.

\Image[paper-2-q12-contours.png]
{title=Exponential level curves and gradient directions for a minimum and a saddle}
{description=Both panels show $|x|,|y|\leq2$. Left: $e^x+e^y-x-y$ has nested <level curves> around its minimum of value two. Right: $e^x-e^y-x+y$ has saddle-shaped <level curves> around value zero. Arrows show normalized <gradient> directions near the origin.}

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Now $h=e^x-e^y-x+y$ and the origin has stationary value zero. Near it,
$$
h=\frac{x^2-y^2}{2}+\frac{x^3-y^3}{6}+O((|x|+|y|)^4).
$$
The <level curves> therefore resemble hyperbolas near the <saddle point of a scalar function>. Along the $x$-axis $h=e^x-x-1>0$ for $x\ne0$, while along the $y$-axis $h=1-e^y+y<0$ for $y\ne0$. The zero <level set> includes the exact diagonal $x=y$ and another branch crossing it at the origin; this second branch is tangent to $y=-x$ but is curved away from the origin. The right panel of the contour figure displays these features.

= d
{parent=12}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For case (i),
$$
\boxed{\nabla h=(e^x-1,e^y-1)^T}.
$$
Each component has the sign of its coordinate, so the <gradient> points outward from the <minimum>. Near the origin it is $(x,y)^T+O(x^2+y^2)$.

For case (ii),
$$
\boxed{\nabla h=(e^x-1,1-e^y)^T}.
$$
Its $x$ component has the sign of $x$, while its $y$ component has the opposite sign to $y$. Thus arrows point outward on the $x$-axis and inward on the $y$-axis, with local form $(x,-y)^T+O(x^2+y^2)$. These directions are included in the contour figure; in both cases the <gradient> is perpendicular to the <level curves> and points toward increasing $h$.

= 13
{parent=Paper 2}
