= a
{parent=13}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

On the first segment, parameterize $\mathbf r_1(t)=(t,t,0)^T$, $0\leq t\leq1$. The <dot products> with $\mathbf r_1'=(1,1,0)^T$ are
$$
\mathbf F(\mathbf r_1)\cdot\mathbf r_1'=2t,\qquad
\mathbf G(\mathbf r_1)\cdot\mathbf r_1'=2t\cos(t^2).
$$
Their <line integrals> are one and $\sin1$, respectively.

On the second segment take $\mathbf r_2(t)=(1,1,t)^T$, with <tangent vector> $(0,0,1)^T$. The corresponding <integrands> are $a-2+2t$ and $c$, whose <integrals> are $a-1$ and $c$. Adding the segments yields
$$
\boxed{\int_{C_1}\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r=a,\qquad
\int_{C_1}\mathbf G\cdot d\mathbf r=\sin1+c}.
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

On the curved path, $\mathbf r(t)=(t,t,t^2)^T$ and $\mathbf r'=(1,1,2t)^T$. Direct substitution gives
$$
\mathbf F(\mathbf r(t))\cdot\mathbf r'(t)=2t+(3a-6)t^2+4t^3,
$$
$$
\mathbf G(\mathbf r(t))\cdot\mathbf r'(t)=2t\cos(t^2)+3ct^2.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{\int_{C_2}\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r=1+(a-2)+1=a},
\qquad
\boxed{\int_{C_2}\mathbf G\cdot d\mathbf r=\sin1+c}.
$$
Both values agree with part (i), for every $a,b,c$. Agreement on these two selected paths alone does not establish a <conservative vector field>.

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $\mathbf F$, all three <curl> components vanish for every $a$. More constructively,
$$
\mathbf F=\nabla\phi_F,\qquad
\phi_F=2x^2+y^2+z^2-2xy-2xz+ayz.
$$
Hence **$\mathbf F$ is conservative for every real $a$**. Its endpoint <vector-field potential> difference is $a$, checking both <line integrals>.

For the other <vector field>, direct <partial differentiation> gives
$$
\nabla\times\mathbf G=2b(1,1,1)^T.
$$
It therefore has a <vector-field potential> exactly when $b=0$, with no restriction on $c$:
$$
\boxed{b=0,\quad c\text{ arbitrary},\qquad
\phi_G=\sin(xy)+cxz+C}.
$$
The value of $a$ is irrelevant to $\mathbf G$. Its rotational part for general $b$ is $b(1,1,1)^T\times\mathbf r$. Both selected paths lie in $x=y$, and this rotational part has zero <dot product> with every <tangent vector> to that <plane>. That explains why these paths cannot detect its nonzero <curl> when $b\ne0$.

