= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

By the <multiplication principle>, the successive places have six, five, four, three, two and one available letters. Thus the number of <permutations> is
$$
\boxed{6!=720}.
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Fixing the last letter leaves five distinct letters to permute freely. Therefore the number is
$$
\boxed{5!=120}.
$$

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The last letter has two permitted choices, and each leaves $5!$ <permutations>. The two classes are disjoint, so the count is
$$
\boxed{2\cdot5!=240}.
$$

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Fixing both specified positions leaves four letters to permute. Hence
$$
\boxed{4!=24}.
$$

= v
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

Let $E_5$ denote the <set> of arrangements with $E$ fifth, and $F_6$ those with $F$ sixth. Each contains $5!$ arrangements, while their <intersection> contains $4!$. The <inclusion-exclusion principle> gives
$$
\boxed{|E_5\cup F_6|=5!+5!-4!=216}.
$$

= vi
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=vi}

Requiring exactly one condition means taking the <symmetric difference> of the two <sets>. Subtract their shared cases twice from the two individual counts:
$$
\boxed{|E_5\mathbin{\triangle}F_6|=5!+5!-2\cdot4!=192}.
$$
Equivalently, remove the twenty-four arrangements satisfying both conditions from the inclusive count $216$.

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The second bin is forced to contain $A,B$, leaving the other four letters in the first bin. Since order within a bin is irrelevant, this gives exactly
$$
\boxed{1}
$$
<set partition> of the specified bin sizes.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The first four letters may be permuted in $4!$ ways and the last two in $2!$ ways without changing this partition. The <multiplication principle> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{4!\,2!=48}.
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Choose the unordered pair of letters in the second bin; the first bin is then determined. The <binomial coefficient> gives
$$
\boxed{\binom62=\frac{6!}{4!\,2!}=15}.
$$
The first and second bins are distinguished, so their roles are not interchangeable.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The product is
$$
\boxed{48\cdot15=720=6!}.
$$
Every <permutation> determines exactly one bin partition, and each such partition has exactly forty-eight internal orderings. Thus the fifteen classes partition the original <sample space> of $720$ arrangements, without overlap or omission.

= v
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

For first-bin size $k$, choose its contents in $\binom6k$ ways. Each choice arises from $k!(6-k)!$ <permutations>, obtained by ordering the two bins internally. The seven ordered size choices give
$$
\begin{array}{c|r|r|r}
\text{bin sizes}&\text{partitions}&\text{orderings per partition}&\text{product}\\\hline
\{0,6\}&1&720&720\\
\{1,5\}&6&120&720\\
\{2,4\}&15&48&720\\
\{3,3\}&20&36&720\\
\{4,2\}&15&48&720\\
\{5,1\}&6&120&720\\
\{6,0\}&1&720&720
\end{array}
$$
Here $0!=1$. Each row expresses the same <binomial coefficient> identity $\binom6k k!(6-k)!=6!$. Even for $\{3,3\}$ the bins retain their first/second labels, so the number is twenty rather than ten; the braces denote bin sizes in the question's convention, not interchangeable bins.

= 15
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <homogeneous solution> is $A\cos x+B\sin x$. If $k\ne1$, the <method of undetermined coefficients> permits a <particular integral> $C\cos kx$. Substitution gives $(1-k^2)C=1$ and hence
$$
\boxed{y(x)=A\cos x+B\sin x+\frac{\cos kx}{1-k^2},\qquad k=2,3,\ldots}.
$$
For $k=1$, the forcing is itself a <homogeneous solution>, so this trial fails by <resonance in a differential equation>. Multiplying the sine trial by $x$ gives
$$
\left(\frac{d^2}{dx^2}+1\right)(x\sin x)=2\cos x.
$$
Thus the resonant <general solution> is
$$
\boxed{y(x)=A\cos x+B\sin x+\frac{x}{2}\sin x,\qquad k=1}.
$$
The <secular term> containing $x$ describes the increasing amplitude under undamped resonant forcing.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Differentiate the first <ordinary differential equation> and substitute the second:
$$
u''=au'+bv'=au'+bcu+bdv.
$$
The original first equation also gives $bv=u'-au$, so
$$
u''=(a+d)u'+(bc-ad)u.
$$
Therefore the requested <coefficients> are
$$
\boxed{\alpha=a+d,\qquad\beta=ad-bc}.
$$
They are the <trace> and <determinant> of the coefficient <matrix>. This derivation never divides by $b$ and is valid even if $b=0$. In that degenerate case the scalar second-order equation is a necessary consequence, while the original constraint $u'=au$ must also be retained when reconstructing the coupled system.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Here $\alpha=4$ and $\beta=4$, so the reduced <ordinary differential equation> is $(D-2)^2u=0$. Its repeated <characteristic root> gives $u=(A+Bt)e^{2t}$. The first <initial condition> sets $A=0$. The original equation $u'=3u-v$ gives $u'(0)=-1$, hence $B=-1$.

Recover $v=3u-u'$ to obtain
$$
\boxed{u(t)=-te^{2t},\qquad v(t)=(1-t)e^{2t}}.
$$
Direct differentiation verifies $v'=u+v$ and both <initial conditions>.

= 16
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The cube is $1\leq x\leq2$, $0\leq y,z\leq1$, so $|x|=x$ throughout it. Evaluate the six outward <surface integrals> directly.

On $x=2$ the normal component is $2$, giving <flux> $2$; on $x=1$ it is $-1$, giving <flux> $-1$. On $y=1$ the normal component is $2x$, giving $\int_1^2\int_0^12x\,dz\,dx=3$; on $y=0$ it is zero. On $z=1$ the normal component is $e^{-1}$ and on $z=0$ it is $-1$, so those unit-area faces give $e^{-1}$ and $-1$.

Adding all six contributions yields
$$
\boxed{\Phi=2-1+3+0+e^{-1}-1=3+e^{-1}}.
$$
Only direct <surface integral> was used, as requested.

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Parameterize the surface by $\mathbf r(x,z)=(x,1+z,z)^T$. Its two <tangent vectors> are $(1,0,0)^T$ and $(0,1,1)^T$, whose <cross product> is $(0,-1,1)^T$ of <Euclidean norm> $\sqrt2$. Thus the scalar <area element of a surface> is
$$
\boxed{dS=\sqrt2\,dx\,dz}.
$$
Equivalently, projection onto the $xz$-<plane> reduces the surface <area> by $|\mathbf n\cdot\mathbf e_y|=1/\sqrt2$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

On the surface $y=1+z$, the <integrand> is $x+z+z^2$. Using the <area element of a surface> from part (i),
$$
\begin{aligned}
\iint_S(x+yz)\,dS
&=\sqrt2\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^1(x+z+z^2)\,dz\,dx\\
&=\sqrt2\int_0^{2\pi}\left(x+\frac56\right)dx\\
&=\boxed{\sqrt2\left(2\pi^2+\frac{5\pi}{3}\right)}.
\end{aligned}
$$

= c
{parent=16}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

On the unit <sphere>, the outward <unit normal> is $\mathbf n=(x,y,z)^T$, so the normal component of the radial <vector field> is $\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf n=x^2+y^2+z^2=1$. The direct <surface integral> is
$$
\Phi=\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^\pi1\cdot\sin\theta\,d\theta\,d\phi
=\boxed{4\pi}.
$$
Here $dS=\sin\theta\,d\theta\,d\phi$ is the unit-sphere <area element of a surface> in <spherical coordinates>.

= ii
