= Solution
{parent=ii}

Direct <matrix multiplication> gives
$$
AP=\begin{pmatrix}2&4\\-1&4\end{pmatrix},\qquad
P^{-1}AP=\frac13\begin{pmatrix}1&-1\\1&2\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}2&4\\-1&4\end{pmatrix}
=\boxed{\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&4\end{pmatrix}}.
$$
Thus $D$ is diagonal. The columns of $P$ are <eigenvectors> of $A$ with <eigenvalues> one and four.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <similarity transformation> gives $A=PDP^{-1}$. In the <matrix power>, each adjacent pair $P^{-1}P$ cancels to the <identity matrix>:
$$
A^n=(PDP^{-1})\cdots(PDP^{-1})
=\boxed{PD^nP^{-1}}.
$$
This also follows by <mathematical induction>: the formula holds for $n=1$, and multiplication by $A=PDP^{-1}$ turns its $n$th instance into the $(n+1)$st.

