= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <volume form> in <cylindrical coordinates> is $dV=r\,dr\,d\phi\,dz$. Integrating around the axis and over the height gives cylindrical-shell <volume> $2\pi hr\,dr$. Hence the <volume integral> is
$$
\boxed{K=\pi h\int_0^a r\,v(r)^2\,dr
=\pi h\int_0^a r^3\omega(r)^2\,dr}.
$$
For unit <density>, this is the <kinetic energy> of the rotational motion.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Using the same cylindrical-shell <volume>,
$$
\boxed{L=2\pi h\int_0^a r^2v(r)\,dr
=2\pi h\int_0^a r^3\omega(r)\,dr}.
$$
For unit <density>, this is the axial <angular momentum>. To bound it, apply the integral <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> to $f(r)=\sqrt r\,v(r)$ and $g(r)=r^{3/2}$:
$$
\left(\int_0^ar^2v(r)\,dr\right)^2
\leq\left(\int_0^arv(r)^2\,dr\right)\left(\int_0^ar^3\,dr\right)
=\frac{K}{\pi h}\frac{a^4}{4}.
$$
Multiplication by $(2\pi h)^2$ gives $L^2\leq\pi ha^4K$. Since the chamber's <volume> is $V=\pi a^2h$, this is precisely
$$
\boxed{K\geq\frac{L^2}{Va^2}}.
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Equality in the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> requires $\sqrt r\,v(r)=\Omega r^{3/2}$ for one constant $\Omega$. Thus $v(r)=\Omega r$ and
$$
\boxed{\omega(r)=\Omega\text{ independent of }r}.
$$
This is <solid-body rotation>. At fixed $L$, the constant is $\Omega=2L/(Va^2)$. The value at the single axis point $r=0$ does not affect the <integrals>; if $\omega$ is continuous, it has the same constant value there as well.

